\section{Additional Results}
\label{app:additional}

\subsection{Top-$k$ Feature Trajectories}
\label{app:topk_trajectories}

Figure~\ref{fig:feature_trajectory} in the main text shows feature trajectories for nucleus ($p{=}0.95$) and temperature ($\tau{=}0.9$) sampling.
Top-$k$ ($k{=}50$) trajectories follow the same monotonically decreasing trend for all features but with compressed inter-depth spacing, consistent with the signal attenuation effect discussed in \S\ref{sec:atlas:strategy}.
Concretely, the d0$\to$d1 drop in \texttt{p90\_ppl} under top-$k$ is 78\% (vs.\ 87\% for nucleus), and the d2$\to$d3 reduction is 31\% (vs.\ 39\% for nucleus).
Despite the magnitude reduction, feature rank ordering across depths is preserved, which explains why ordinal classifiers maintain directionality even under top-$k$ conditions.

\subsection{Permutation Importance}
\label{app:permutation}

To verify that the Gini-based importance rankings in \S\ref{sec:atlas:features} are not an artifact of the impurity measure, we compute permutation importance (5-fold CV, 10 repeats) for all 9 atlas cells.
The top-2 features are stable across measures: \texttt{p90\_ppl} ranks in the top 2 in 9/9 cells under both Gini and permutation importance, and \texttt{var\_surprisal} in 8/9 (vs.\ 7/9 for Gini, with the additional cell being OLMo top-$k$; the single SHAP exception is GPT-2~XL top-$k$, where \texttt{p75\_ppl} displaces \texttt{var\_surprisal} to rank 3).
Kendall's $W{=}0.90$ across all three methods (Gini, permutation, SHAP) indicates strong concordance ($p{<}0.005$).
Pairwise Spearman correlations are: SHAP--Gini $\rho{=}0.93$, SHAP--permutation $\rho{=}0.84$, Gini--permutation $\rho{=}0.76$.
Per-cell top-5 overlap between SHAP and permutation importance is 3--4 out of 5 features; the aggregated (mean-across-cells) top-5 sets are identical; the top features are therefore robust to the choice of importance measure at the aggregate level, though per-cell rankings exhibit model-dependent variation.

\subsection{Per-Class Recall}
\label{app:per_class}

Table~\ref{tab:per_class_recall} reports per-class recall across all 9 atlas cells (4-class in-distribution, 5-fold CV).
Depth~0 recall exceeds 98\% uniformly.
Among generated depths, d2 is the hardest class in every cell; the deficit is most pronounced under top-$k$ sampling (46--53\%), where vocabulary truncation removes the tail tokens that carry depth-discriminative signal.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\caption{Per-class recall (\%) for all 9 atlas cells. d2 is the lowest-recall class in every cell. Boldface marks the highest d2 recall.}
\label{tab:per_class_recall}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccc@{}}
\toprule
Cell & d0 & d1 & d2 & d3 \\
\midrule
Pythia, nuc.       & 99.7 & 81.9 & 58.7 & 72.3 \\
Pythia, $\tau$     & 99.7 & 81.3 & 53.9 & 72.1 \\
Pythia, top-$k$    & 99.5 & 72.3 & 45.6 & 57.5 \\
OLMo, nuc.         & 98.8 & 80.5 & 61.5 & 74.3 \\
OLMo, $\tau$       & 99.7 & 87.7 & 66.1 & 77.2 \\
OLMo, top-$k$      & 99.5 & 78.5 & 51.2 & 65.6 \\
GPT-2~XL, nuc.     & 99.2 & 85.9 & \textbf{73.3} & 77.5 \\
GPT-2~XL, $\tau$   & 99.5 & 90.9 & 68.8 & 81.9 \\
GPT-2~XL, top-$k$  & 98.5 & 79.5 & 52.5 & 71.1 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{DCOL Iteration Details}

\section{The Depth Degradation Atlas}
\label{sec:atlas}

We present the Depth Degradation Atlas: a systematic empirical map of how statistical fingerprints evolve across recursive generation depths, models, and decoding strategies.
The atlas spans $3 \times 3 = 9$ model--strategy cells evaluated at depths $d \in \{0, 1, 2, 3\}$, with depth extensions to $d{=}5$ for cross-model EDD validation (\S\ref{sec:atlas:edd}).

%% ----------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{In-Distribution Performance}
\label{sec:atlas:id}

Table~\ref{tab:main_matrix} reports four-class Random Forest accuracy (5-fold CV, 200 trees) across all nine cells.
Accuracy ranges from 68.8\% (Pythia, top-$k$) to 85.9\% (GPT-2~XL, temperature), all exceeding the 25\% chance baseline by a wide margin.
Two axis effects emerge.
GPT-2~XL achieves the highest accuracy under every strategy (75.9--85.9\%), followed by OLMo (74.5--82.1\%) and Pythia (68.8--78.7\%).
Top-$k$ sampling yields the lowest accuracy in every model.
The mean within-model strategy gap is 9.2~pp, comparable to the 7.2~pp mean within-strategy model gap; decoding strategy and model family contribute roughly equally to performance variance.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Four-class RF accuracy (\%) with bootstrap 95\% CI (${\pm}$half-width; 10{,}000 resamples) across the $3 \times 3$ atlas; d1--d3 balanced accuracy in parentheses (chance = 25\% / 33\%). Depth-0 recall exceeds 98\% in all cells. Within-distribution ordinal quality: MAE\,=\,0.23, QWK\,=\,0.89 (QWK$_{\text{d1--d3}}$\,=\,0.74); 98.2\% of errors fall on adjacent depths ($|\Delta| \leq 1$).}
\label{tab:main_matrix}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lccc@{}}
\toprule
& Nucleus & Temp.\ $\tau{=}0.9$ & Top-$k$ \\
\midrule
Pythia-1.4B  & 78.7{\scriptsize$\pm$0.5}\,(71.2) & 76.7{\scriptsize$\pm$0.5}\,(70.6) & 68.8{\scriptsize$\pm$0.6}\,(58.8) \\
OLMo-1B     & 78.9{\scriptsize$\pm$0.5}\,(75.0) & 82.1{\scriptsize$\pm$0.5}\,(76.3) & 74.5{\scriptsize$\pm$0.5}\,(66.1) \\
GPT-2~XL    & \textbf{84.1}{\scriptsize$\pm$0.5}\,(79.0) & \textbf{85.9}{\scriptsize$\pm$0.5}\,(82.6) & \textbf{75.9}{\scriptsize$\pm$0.6}\,(69.5) \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Per-class recall.}
Depth-0 recall exceeds 98\% in all 9 cells.
Among generated depths, d2 consistently yields the lowest recall (46--73\%), followed by d3 (58--82\%) and d1 (72--91\%).
The d2 trough reflects its position at the center of the depth gradient, where features overlap with both d1 and d3, consistent with d2-vs-d3 as the EDD bottleneck (\S\ref{sec:atlas:edd}).
Full per-class recall appears in Appendix~\ref{app:per_class}.

%% ----------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Feature Consistency Across the Grid}
\label{sec:atlas:features}

Despite the 17.1~pp accuracy spread across cells, a small feature family dominates in every setting.
Perplexity tail statistics as a feature group dominate depth discrimination: the 90th-percentile perplexity (\texttt{p90\_ppl}) ranks top-2 by Gini importance in all 9 cells; surprisal variance (\texttt{var\_surprisal}) in 8/9 under SHAP (7/9 under Gini; Appendix~\ref{app:permutation}).\footnote{Individual feature rankings should be interpreted cautiously given high multicollinearity (VIF${>}$10 for 14--16/19 features); the group-level dominance of perplexity tail statistics is robust across importance measures.}
Table~\ref{tab:feature_importance} shows the full per-cell ranking.

Cross-model feature-rank Spearman correlation averages $\bar{\rho} = 0.868$ (range $[0.619, 0.954]$): models agree on which features carry depth information even when they differ in absolute accuracy.
Among strategies, top-$k$ yields the highest cross-model rank correlation ($\rho = 0.811$): the deterministic vocabulary truncation imposes a more uniform feature landscape across architectures, at the cost of weaker overall signal (\S\ref{sec:atlas:strategy}).

Feature ablation confirms this concentration: the top-5 features achieve 97.6\% of full accuracy (76.5\% vs.\ 78.4\%), with perplexity statistics alone reaching 76.6\% (vs.\ surprisal 73.2\%, other categories ${\leq}$47\%).

The feature hierarchy shifts with depth.
At shallow boundaries (d1 vs.\ d2), \texttt{var\_surprisal} dominates importance; at deeper boundaries (d3 vs.\ d4, d4 vs.\ d5), tail perplexity statistics (\texttt{p90\_ppl}, \texttt{kurtosis\_ppl}) take over.
This transition aligns with model-collapse dynamics~\cite{shumailov2024_nature}: early recursion narrows the surprisal distribution (captured by its variance), while later steps erode the perplexity tail (captured by upper quantiles and kurtosis).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Top-5 features by Gini importance. \textbf{Bold}: top-2. \texttt{p90\_ppl} top-2 in 9/9; \texttt{var\_surprisal} 8/9 under SHAP (7/9 under Gini).}
\label{tab:feature_importance}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{@{}l l@{}}
\toprule
Cell & Top-5 features (Gini importance, ranked) \\
\midrule
Pythia, nuc.       & \textbf{var\_surp\,(.17)}, \textbf{p90\_ppl\,(.15)}, m\_ppl\,(.11), var\_ppl\,(.08), p75\,(.07) \\
Pythia, $\tau$     & \textbf{var\_surp\,(.17)}, \textbf{p90\_ppl\,(.14)}, m\_ppl\,(.11), p75\,(.07), m\_surp\,(.07) \\
Pythia, top-$k$    & \textbf{var\_surp\,(.16)}, \textbf{p90\_ppl\,(.12)}, m\_ppl\,(.11), var\_ppl\,(.07), m\_surp\,(.06) \\
GPT-2\,XL, nuc.    & \textbf{var\_surp\,(.21)}, \textbf{p90\_ppl\,(.21)}, p75\,(.10), m\_surp\,(.07), m\_ppl\,(.06) \\
GPT-2\,XL, $\tau$  & \textbf{p90\_ppl\,(.25)}, \textbf{p75\,(.15)}, m\_surp\,(.13), var\_surp\,(.12), p50\,(.05) \\
GPT-2\,XL, top-$k$ & \textbf{p90\_ppl\,(.24)}, \textbf{p75\,(.12)}, var\_surp\,(.10), m\_surp\,(.09), ttr\,(.04) \\
OLMo, nuc.         & \textbf{p90\_ppl\,(.18)}, \textbf{var\_surp\,(.15)}, p75\,(.11), m\_surp\,(.07), ttr\,(.06) \\
OLMo, $\tau$       & \textbf{p90\_ppl\,(.21)}, \textbf{var\_surp\,(.16)}, p75\,(.12), m\_surp\,(.09), ttr\,(.05) \\
OLMo, top-$k$      & \textbf{p90\_ppl\,(.20)}, \textbf{var\_surp\,(.17)}, p75\,(.10), m\_surp\,(.07), m\_ppl\,(.05) \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_atlas_heatmap.pdf}
\caption{Atlas feature importance heatmap. Rows: 19 features grouped by category (\S\ref{sec:method:features}); columns: 9 model--strategy cells. Color intensity encodes RF Gini importance. The perplexity-tail and surprisal-variance blocks maintain high importance across all cells.}
\label{fig:atlas_heatmap}
\end{figure}

%% ----------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Monotonicity and Inter-Depth Distances}
\label{sec:atlas:monotonicity}

Depth features exhibit strong monotonic trends: perplexity tail statistics (\texttt{p90\_ppl}, \texttt{mean\_surprisal}) decrease with depth (Spearman $\rho = -1.0$ across cell-level medians) while repetition features increase. This pattern reflects the complementary narrowing and self-repetition of distribution collapse.
Inter-depth distances are non-uniform: d0-vs-d1 AUC exceeds 0.99 in all 9 cells, whereas d2-vs-d3 ranges from 0.69 to 0.89; separating adjacent deep generations requires the tail statistics identified in \S\ref{sec:atlas:features}.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
